## 2. Noncommercial and Commercial Use

“Noncommercial Use” means use whose primary purpose and actual function are education, learning, reproducibility, independent scientific investigation, or dissemination of knowledge, without being part of Commercial Use below. Salary, a user's legal form, ordinary academic tuition, and the mere presence or absence of a fee are not individually determinative. Labels such as “research”, “nonprofit”, “pilot” or “free” do not override the actual function, contractual obligations and intended beneficiaries.

“Commercial Use” means any use of Covered Software, including modifications, to:

(a) develop, evaluate for adoption, integrate, operate, market, sell, license or provide a commercial product or service, including a free feature supporting a commercial offering;
(b) provide a paid deployment, managed service, customer implementation or consulting deliverable using the software; payment solely for research dissemination or cost recovery is addressed separately below and is not automatically a commercial deployment;
(c) perform an organization's operational business functions, such as production decisions, employee/customer analytics, revenue operations or internal business automation, even where the software is not sold or exposed to customers;
(d) carry out commissioned research primarily intended to satisfy a customer's business, product or proprietary operational objective, including a paid proprietary feasibility study, exclusive customer deliverable or product-development contract; or
(e) use the software in a workflow to create or improve a model, feature, report or other deliverable primarily for any of the commercial purposes in (a)–(d).

Independent academic research and reproducibility for knowledge dissemination are Noncommercial Use when they do not serve those commercial functions. A company-employed researcher can qualify under that test; a university or nonprofit carrying out commercial commissioned work may not. Funding alone does not determine the result. Mixed-purpose use qualifies as Noncommercial Use only for a genuinely separable noncommercial activity; the commercial activity requires authorization. No permission is implied for unresolved mixed-purpose cases.

A later commercial deployment or commercial development activity using Covered Software requires prior written authorization even if an earlier independent research activity was noncommercial. This clause does not assert copyright in abstract ideas, algorithms, facts, published scientific findings, or independently created implementations. It does not automatically impose this license on every output merely because software was used to produce it. Separate rights in datasets, weights or other outputs must be assessed under their own terms; the commercial workflow restriction above governs use of Covered Software itself.

